      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently looking at a collection of ceramic sherds from a Middle Bronze Age stratum. I'm having some trouble distinguishing between vessel types based solely on the firing techniques used. Could you explain the primary ways I can differentiate between oxidized and reduced firing in the clay fabric and the surface slip?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly. In ceramics, oxidized firing occurs in the presence of abundant oxygen, usually resulting in a brighter, thinner glaze due to the greater availability of oxygen to react with the clay minerals. You might notice that vessels from oxidized firings often have more luster and less color variation. In contrast, reduced firing, which has fewer oxygen levels, tends to cause darker, thicker, and redder glazes due to the conversion of iron impurities into iron-rich minerals. Surface slips in oxidized firing are often smoother and glossier, whereas reduced slip might show a more matte and uniform appearance due to the less volatile interactions with the clay body's silica. By examining these characteristics, you can infer which firing technique was used.",
